"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 7.93 and the pathological finding of positive surgical margins. The Gleason score was documented as 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) on the prostatectomy specimen, which contributes to risk assessment. A countervailing finding is the absence of extraprostatic extension and no seminal vesicle invasion. Notably, no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty regarding prognosis. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the initial biopsy are missing. Future prognostic refinement would benefit significantly from postoperative PSA measurements and tracking its subsequent trajectory. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.77 months; its coverage has not been validated."
